Imagine a different package on which honest access to the medical risk sits beside another possible branch. One version could show the ocean without promising that quitting tomorrow will cause the universe to deliver Hawaii, because humanity has suffered enough motivational coaching without giving it a government procurement contract. A simple piece of arithmetic would be enough: spending around ten euros a day amounts to roughly €3,650 a year, and someone once spent a similar sum on a journey.

That is not a guarantee, a moral verdict, or a promise of paradise. It simply makes another branch of reality visible beside the cigarette. Another pack might show an ordinary person stepping outside for five minutes without smoking, not a wellness athlete who has already run a half marathon, drunk celery, and forgiven their father before sunrise, but someone normal enough for the message to remain credible: **the break is still yours**.

This is a fundamentally different psychology. The old model shows what to run from, while the new one also shows somewhere to go; the consequence does not disappear, but it stops being the only door the interface permits the person to see. In the earlier essay *The Human in a Room That Answers*, we argued that freedom among twenty visible doors is weaker than it appears if the architecture has hidden the twenty-first.

Applied to behaviour, this becomes a right to see alternatives in the same temporal scale in which the choice is being made. Not only “you may be healthier in twenty years,” but “here is what you can do with these five minutes now.” We can call this an **alternative aperture**: the system does not conceal danger, but it refuses to let danger monopolize the entire image of what comes next.

## Do not remove the need together with the behaviour

A deeper principle follows. If we want to change behaviour, it is worth understanding **what human function the behaviour is performing**. If the cigarette provided a break, removing the cigarette does not automatically create a break; if alcohol temporarily muted anxiety, removing alcohol does not teach a person how to live with anxiety; if the phone was the only place where a teenager had company, removing the phone does not create friends. Prohibiting a form does not abolish the need that was using that form.

Human systems are strangely fond of behavioural amputation. They see an unwanted action, cut it off, declare the bug fixed, and are surprised when the need grows back somewhere else a week later. The need never received the memo informing it that it had been cancelled. A more humane system tries to preserve a legitimate function while finding it another body, so the break can remain while the cigarette disappears, social contact can remain while the ritual changes, and the need for regulation can remain real while the means of regulation becomes different.

This is no longer the morality of “do not do the bad thing.” It is behavioural architecture. It does not begin with the assumption that people do foolish things simply because they are foolish; it asks what work the foolish thing is performing in a life. Sometimes the answer is inconvenient for the institution itself, because it means the person is not the only thing that needs repair.

## Why large systems like punitive care

At this point the cigarette pack finally stops being only a cigarette pack. The same mechanism scales into bureaucracy, education, social policy, medicine, work, digital platforms, and family life, because it is almost always cheaper for a large system to tell a person what not to do than to understand why they are doing it. Warnings and sanctions scale beautifully; context fits poorly into a standard form.

It is easy to send an unemployed person a warning about noncompliance and much harder to build a realistic path from their present situation to work they can actually perform. It is easy to send a debtor a red letter and harder to make a comprehensible route out of debt; it is easy to tell someone with obesity to lose weight and harder to see sleep, stress, income, medication, food access, habits, environment, and the realistic range of change. It is easy to tell a teenager to use the phone less and much harder to help create an offline life worth returning to.

Punitive care is attractive because it is cheap, scalable, and remarkably presentation-friendly. Write the rule, print the warning, add the sanction, place a “we care” logo in the corner, watch the KPI turn green, and lunch can proceed on schedule. Genuine care is more expensive because it asks why the person arrived here in the first place, and sometimes the answer is uncomfortable not for the individual but for the room the system built around them.

## Sometimes the room needs repair

People are told to manage stress while their workplace produces stress at industrial scale; they are told to eat well while economic and urban environments make some food cheaper, faster, and easier to reach than other food. They are told to spend less time on social platforms while those platforms professionally optimize attention retention, and told to be financially responsible while parts of the credit economy profit precisely from short time horizons and impulsive decisions. The system then looks at the person standing inside all this architecture and solemnly says, “make the right choice.”

None of this abolishes personal responsibility. It exists, but it should not become a washing machine for the responsibility of the environment, because freedom behaves differently in rooms with different architecture. *The Interface That Knows You* already made a parallel point about digital design: an interface does not merely present options, it shapes what appears first, what looks large, what receives a notification, and what quietly remains below the fold; physical and institutional environments perform similar work.

Sometimes the problem is not only that a person made a poor choice. Sometimes the room has spent years making one door cheaper, faster, more socially convenient, or psychologically available than the others, then demanded an individual moral triumph over its own architecture. Humanism that never sees the room can become a very polished service for repairing citizens.

## The right to see the consequence

This is where the original idea can be turned around. Perhaps the answer is not to show people **fewer** consequences but to make consequences much more visible, while refusing to equate visibility with compulsory exposure to the most horrifying possible image. There is a large difference between the right to know and the obligation to look at a photograph of death every time you take a product out of your pocket.

From that distinction emerges another design, a **Consequence Passport**, a digital layer that does not judge the person but makes it possible to see a product’s provenance, well-established benefits and risks, weaker or disputed evidence, material and social tail, end of life, and realistic alternatives. This idea should not be confused with an existing regulatory standard; it is an ethical proposal rather than the official name of a European system. Yet the technical architecture is no longer science fiction.

On [20 July 2026, the European Commission launched the Digital Product Passport Registry](https://single-market-economy.ec.europa.eu/news/digital-product-passport-registry-now-live-2026-07-20_en), and the DPP is described as a digital container for product information intended to improve supply-chain transparency and informed choices. It is not our Consequence Passport and not a finished moral-navigation system, but it demonstrates that physical products can realistically acquire an accessible digital continuation carrying information about provenance and life cycle. The infrastructure is becoming possible; the open question is what kind of meaning and governance we place inside it.

A code on a pack, car, jacket, or other object could lead neither to a corporate page where a green leaf embraces the logo nor to a government page shouting in red type. It could lead to a map of participation where benefit, risk, provenance, strength of evidence, disagreement, uncertainty, and realistic ways to change one’s involvement remain visible in the same space. Such a map does not turn the buyer into a defendant; it simply refuses to let the product exist without a past or a future.

## From guilt to participation

Traditional morality loves the question “who is guilty?” and sometimes that question is indispensable, but if it becomes the only instrument, the world quickly turns into an endless courtroom. Another question is often more useful for everyday ethics: **what am I participating in?** It neither makes a person responsible for the entire world nor permits them to declare every invisible consequence somebody else’s problem.

A smartphone buyer is not personally responsible for every mine, an airline passenger does not control the global energy system, and a smoker did not invent the tobacco industry. The opposite extreme is equally strange: if I cannot see the consequence from my window, it no longer has anything to do with me. Between total guilt and total innocence lies a more adult space in which a person can lack control over the entire chain and still see the thread that connects their action to it.
